--- import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro"; import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro"; import "../../styles/standards-poster.css"; import "../../styles/extended.css"; import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj14.json"; --- <StandardPageLayout code={metadata.code} standardType={metadata.framework} title={metadata.title} description={metadata.description} issued={metadata.issued} latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment} status={metadata.status} summary={metadata.summary} bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice} > <Fragment slot="toc"> <PageToc items={[{"id":"s1","label":"Conceptual Framework Linkage"},{"id":"s2","label":"Scope — What Part 2 Historically Addressed"},{"id":"s3","label":"Continuing the Staged Reform From Statement No. 3"},{"id":"s4","label":"Moving Toward Fuller Balance Sheet Recognition"},{"id":"s5","label":"Actuarial Gains/Losses and Prior Service Cost — the Direction of Travel"},{"id":"s6","label":"Why It Was Abolished — Absorption Into ASBJ Statement No. 26"},{"id":"s7","label":"Worked Example — the Reform's Direction in Outline"},{"id":"s8","label":"Current Treatment — ASBJ Statement No. 26"},{"id":"s9","label":"Disclosure Requirements (Current, Post-Abolition)"},{"id":"s10","label":"Critical Judgments"},{"id":"s11","label":"Relationship to IAS/IFRS — the IAS 19 Parallel"},{"id":"s12","label":"Statement Mapping"},{"id":"s13","label":"Related Standards"},{"id":"s14","label":"Glossary"}]} /> </Fragment> <div class="wrap"> <div class="callout warn">ABOLISHED STATUS: ASBJ Statement No. 14 was the second stage of Japan's staged retirement benefits reform (following No. 3, and preceding No. 19). It has since been ABOLISHED (廃止) and fully absorbed into the comprehensive ASBJ Statement No. 26, Accounting Standard for Retirement Benefits. This poster is retained for historical reference, continuing the account begun in the companion ASBJ Statement No. 3 poster.</div> <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The second stage of a three-stage retirement benefits reform</div><div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 14 represented the SECOND stage (Part 2) of Japan's INCREMENTAL retirement benefits reform — following EARLIER Statement No. 3 (Part 1) and PRECEDING Statement No. 19 (Part 3) — before the ENTIRE staged process CULMINATED in the comprehensive <strong>ASBJ Statement No. 26</strong>. Like Statement No. 3, it has since been <strong>ABOLISHED</strong>, its substance FULLY absorbed into the current, comprehensive standard.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — What Part 2 Historically Addressed</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Companies sponsoring retirement benefit plans</div><div class="principle">Like the OTHER stages of this staged reform, Statement No. 14 HISTORICALLY applied to companies sponsoring RETIREMENT BENEFIT plans — both defined benefit pension arrangements and Japan's distinctively COMMON lump-sum retirement allowance plans — for their employees.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Continuing the Staged Reform From Statement No. 3</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Building incrementally toward a comprehensive standard</div><div class="text-supporting">Where Statement No. 3 addressed an EARLY stage of retirement benefit reform, Statement No. 14 REPRESENTED a FURTHER, more DEVELOPED stage — REFINING and EXTENDING the reform's scope, WITHOUT yet reaching the FULL, comprehensive framework that would eventually arrive with ASBJ Statement No. 26. This INCREMENTAL, multi-stage approach — reforming a complex TOPIC area through SUCCESSIVE partial amendments rather than a single wholesale rewrite — is a DISTINCTIVE, recurring feature of J-GAAP standard-setting history (see the companion ASBJ Statement No. 3 poster for the FULLER context of this pattern).</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Moving Toward Fuller Balance Sheet Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A general direction shared with international reform of the same period</div><div class="text-supporting">As part of the BROADER, MULTI-STAGE reform direction, this stage of the process moved Japanese practice TOWARD FULLER recognition of a company's NET retirement benefit obligation DIRECTLY on the Balance Sheet — REDUCING reliance on OFF-BALANCE-SHEET tracking of items such as UNRECOGNISED actuarial gains/losses and unrecognised PRIOR service cost, which had previously been permitted to remain UNRECOGNISED for extended periods under older Japanese practice.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Actuarial Gains/Losses and Prior Service Cost — the Direction of Travel</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Reducing deferral, moving toward more immediate recognition</div><div class="text-supporting">The STAGED reform progressively MOVED Japanese practice AWAY from DEFERRED recognition mechanisms for ACTUARIAL gains/losses and PRIOR SERVICE COST (analogous in SPIRIT to the "corridor approach" once used internationally under an EARLIER version of IAS 19) TOWARD recognising these items MORE FULLY and MORE PROMPTLY within Net Assets and/or the Statement of Income — a DIRECTION OF TRAVEL that MIRRORS the SAME broad shift the IASB itself made through its OWN 2011 amendments to IAS 19, which ELIMINATED the corridor approach INTERNATIONALLY around a broadly SIMILAR period.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Why It Was Abolished — Absorption Into ASBJ Statement No. 26</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Consolidation into a single, comprehensive standard</div><div class="text-supporting">Once the ASBJ completed the FULL, comprehensive reform of retirement benefit accounting — culminating in ASBJ Statement No. 26 — the EARLIER, INCREMENTAL partial amendments (Statement No. 3, Statement No. 14, and eventually much of Statement No. 19) became REDUNDANT, their substance having been FULLY incorporated and further refined within the SINGLE comprehensive standard. Statement No. 14 was therefore <strong>ABOLISHED (廃止)</strong>, consistent with GOOD standard-setting practice.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — the Reform's Direction in Outline</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">From deferred, off-balance-sheet tracking to fuller recognition</div> <div class="worked"><div class="wk-title">Example: Unrecognised actuarial losses, before and after the reform</div><div class="wk-body">A company sponsoring a defined benefit plan had, under OLDER practice, a significant balance of UNRECOGNISED actuarial LOSSES being deferred and amortised gradually over future periods, rather than recognised immediately.</div><div class="wk-answer">Through the STAGED reform (including this Statement's contribution), Japanese practice MOVED toward recognising SUCH amounts MORE FULLY and PROMPTLY — a direction that CULMINATED, under TODAY's ASBJ Statement No. 26, in a framework where the NET retirement benefit LIABILITY (or asset) recognised on the Balance Sheet reflects a FAR MORE COMPLETE picture of the underlying obligation than the OLDER, more DEFERRAL-ORIENTED practice had provided.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Current Treatment — ASBJ Statement No. 26</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Today's authoritative retirement benefits framework</div><div class="text-supporting">Companies TODAY apply <strong>ASBJ Statement No. 26</strong> in its ENTIRETY, addressing recognition and measurement of retirement benefit obligations, plan assets, actuarial gains/losses, and prior service cost — the CURRENT, comprehensive successor incorporating the FULL substance of Statements No. 3, No. 14, and No. 19's staged reform history.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Current, Post-Abolition)</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What applies today instead</div><div class="check-list"><span>Full compliance with ASBJ Statement No. 26's recognition, measurement, and disclosure requirements</span><span>No separate reference to the now-abolished Statement No. 14 is required or appropriate</span></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most (historically and today)</div> <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">HISTORICAL JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Correctly sequencing application of the staged partial amendments during the reform period</span></div></div> <div><div class="elabel">CURRENT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Actuarial assumptions and measurement judgements now governed entirely by ASBJ Statement No. 26</span></div></div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS — the IAS 19 Parallel</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A shared international direction of travel</div><div class="text-supporting">Japan's STAGED move away from deferred actuarial gain/loss recognition BROADLY PARALLELS the IASB's OWN 2011 amendments to IAS 19, which ELIMINATED the "corridor approach" internationally, requiring immediate recognition of REMEASUREMENTS through Other Comprehensive Income. While the SPECIFIC mechanics and TIMING of Japan's staged reform DIFFER from the IASB's OWN single amendment, the underlying DIRECTION — toward FULLER, more IMMEDIATE Balance Sheet recognition of retirement benefit obligations — reflects a broadly SHARED international standard-setting trend of the SAME period.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where retirement benefit accounting now lands</div><div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div> <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Retirement benefit obligation and plan assets</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Governed entirely by the current ASBJ Statement No. 26</span></div> </div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div><div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div> <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 3</span><span>Part 1 of the same staged reform history — also now abolished</span></div> <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 26</span><span>Successor, comprehensive standard — the current authoritative J-GAAP retirement benefits framework</span></div> </div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cn span2"> <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Unrecognised actuarial gains/losses (legacy concept)</div><div class="glossary-def">Actuarial gains/losses historically permitted to remain deferred off the Balance Sheet, before the staged reform moved toward fuller, more immediate recognition.</div></div> <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Abolished (廃止)</div><div class="glossary-def">The formal ASBJ status indicating a standard's substance has been fully absorbed into a successor standard, with the original no longer separately in force.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</span></div></div> </div> </StandardPageLayout> 